# Occupations, as the registers give them — not as the family tree gives them.
#
# The tree records an occupation for 16 of 1,245 published people. The registers
# record one for almost everybody, because the Latin and Croatian parish forms
# both carry a *conditio* or *stalež* column, and the priest filled it in for
# parents, godparents and witnesses alike.
#
# So this is a column the archive has been reading past. Where an occupation has
# appeared in these pages it has gone into prose on one person's page and nowhere
# else, which means it cannot be counted, compared or mapped. This file is the
# start of fixing that.
#
# Each row cites the entry it came from. Nothing here is inferred.
#
# WHICH REGISTERS CARRY THIS COLUMN — triaged 13 September 2026.
# It is not a question of language, which is what I assumed. It is the FORM.
#
#   Ruled tabular registers HAVE a conditio / stalez column:
#     Otocac 1834, 1835, 1845 (Latin)   — every parent AND godparent gets a
#                                          trade or a rank. The richest by far.
#     Novi Vinodolski 1894-1900 (Croat) — parents yes, godparents usually not.
#     Senj 1886-1891 (Croatian)         — parents and godparents, briefly.
#     Sv. Jakov Krmpote 1882-1895       — mostly just "seljaci".
#
#   Prose registers have NO column at all:
#     Karlobag 1754-1781 (Latin) — continuous Latin sentences, no ruling. Six of
#     the eight Latin documents this archive holds are these, and reading them
#     for occupations is wasted effort.
#
# So of 37 held documents only THREE are Otocac tabular, and two are now read.
#
# person | occupation (as written) | in English | family | place | year | source
